---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { SITE, MAINTAINER } from '../lib/meta';

const faq = [
  ['What does enshittification mean?',
   'Cory Doctorow coined the word in 2022. It describes how platforms decay: first they are good to their users, then they squeeze users to favour business customers, then they squeeze business customers to keep the value for themselves.'],
  ['Does enshittification apply to hardware?',
   'Yes. Hardware is sold once, but its software keeps changing. Home screens fill with ads, cloud services shut down, apps get abandoned and operating system support ends. The device still works physically while the software makes it worse.'],
  ['Can you undo enshittification on a device you own?',
   'Often, partly. People replace launchers, write local servers for devices whose cloud died, write missing drivers and install new operating systems. Each entry on unshittify lists what the fix restores and what still does not work.'],
  ['Is it legal to modify a device you own?',
   'It depends on where you live, the device terms and the software licences involved. Owning a device is a strong starting point but not a blanket answer. unshittify lists owner-controlled projects only and does not offer legal advice.'],
  ['Do I need to be a programmer?',
   'Not always. Coding assistants such as Claude Code now let owners read unfamiliar code, decode protocols and build replacements. Some projects still need soldering or command-line work. Pick one you can undo.'],
] as const;

// Dated hardware examples, newest first. Every fact here comes from the linked
// entry's write-up, so the entry is the source. Add new ones at the top.
const examples = [
  { when: 'Sep 2026', device: 'June Oven', what: 'Weber shut down the June app and cloud on 22 September 2026, taking preheat, cook programs and the camera with it.', fix: 'Project July', slug: 'project-july-june-oven' },
  { when: 'May 2026', device: 'Bose SoundTouch speakers', what: 'Bose shut down the SoundTouch cloud. Internet radio and the six preset buttons stopped working.', fix: 'Bose SoundTouch revival', slug: 'bose-soundtouch-revival' },
  { when: 'Jan 2026', device: 'Belkin Wemo plugs and switches', what: 'Belkin shut down the Wemo cloud on 31 January 2026. Devices can no longer be set up through the Wemo app.', fix: 'Bit Switch', slug: 'bit-switch-wemo' },
  { when: 'Oct 2025', device: 'Nest Learning Thermostat, gen 1 and 2', what: 'Google ended support on 25 October 2025. The app, remote control and voice control stopped, leaving manual thermostats.', fix: 'NoLongerEvil', slug: 'nolongerevil-nest-thermostat' },
  { when: 'Apr 2025', device: 'Fisker Ocean', what: 'After Fisker’s bankruptcy, the connection to its cloud went down and the features that depended on it stopped.', fix: 'Fisker Owners Association', slug: 'fisker-owners-association' },
  { when: 'Feb 2025', device: 'Humane Ai Pin', what: 'Humane shut down the Pin’s servers, and the assistant that was the whole point of the device went with them.', fix: 'OpenPin', slug: 'openpin' },
  { when: 'Early 2025', device: 'Moxie robot', what: 'Embodied’s cloud went dark and the US$800 companion robot for children stopped working.', fix: 'OpenMoxie', slug: 'openmoxie' },
  { when: 'Dec 2024', device: 'Spotify Car Thing', what: 'Spotify ended Car Thing service and told owners to throw the devices away.', fix: 'DeskThing', slug: 'deskthing-car-thing' },
  { when: 'Oct 2024', device: 'Enel X JuiceBox EV chargers', what: 'Enel X Way announced it was leaving North America nine days later. The chargers kept charging; the app, scheduling and monitoring went with the servers.', fix: 'JuicePass Proxy', slug: 'juicepassproxy' },
  { when: 'Ongoing', device: 'Hisense VIDAA TVs', what: 'The TV boots into an ad-filled home screen and keeps sending telemetry with every toggle off.', fix: 'alvidaa', slug: 'alvidaa-hisense-vidaa' },
  { when: 'Ongoing', device: 'NordicTrack treadmills', what: 'The treadmill’s own screen nags for an iFit subscription before you can simply run.', fix: 'STRIDE', slug: 'stride-nordictrack-without-ifit' },
];

const jsonld = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'Article',
    headline: 'Enshittification: meaning, hardware examples, and how people undo it',
    url: `${SITE}/enshittification/`,
    author: MAINTAINER,
    publisher: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'unshittify', url: SITE },
    dateModified: '2026-11-05',
    about: { '@type': 'Thing', name: 'Enshittification', sameAs: 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Enshittification' },
  },
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'FAQPage',
    mainEntity: faq.map(([q, a]) => ({ '@type': 'Question', name: q, acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: a } })),
  },
];
---
<Base
  title="Enshittification: meaning, hardware examples, and how to undo it | unshittify"
  description="Enshittification, the word Cory Doctorow coined for how platforms decay, applied to hardware you own: ad-filled TV home screens, dead clouds, abandoned apps, ended OS support. Plus real projects that undo it."
  ogType="article"
  jsonld={jsonld}>
<article class="wrap legal">
  <p class="prompt"><a href="/" class="dim">~</a><span class="acc">/</span>enshittification</p>
  <h1 class="detail-h">Enshittification</h1>
  <p class="note">What it means for the hardware you own, and how people undo it. Updated 2026-11-05.</p>

  <div class="prose legal-body">
    <p class="def"><b>Enshittification</b> is the way products and platforms get worse for the people who use them once those people are locked in. With hardware it happens through software: ads on the home screen, a cloud that shuts down, an app that stops working, a subscription for features you already paid for.</p>
    <p>Writer <a href="https://pluralistic.net/">Cory Doctorow</a> coined <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Enshittification">enshittification</a> in 2022 to describe how online platforms decay. First they are good to users. Then they squeeze users to please business customers. Then they squeeze the business customers too. The word stuck because people recognised it everywhere. In 2025 he published the book on it, <a href="https://craphound.com/category/enshittification/"><cite>Enshittification: Why Everything Suddenly Got Worse and What to Do About It</cite></a> (MCD/Farrar, Straus and Giroux).</p>
    <p>Hardware gets the same treatment. You pay once, but the software on the device keeps changing after you have paid, and the changes don't always favour you. The plastic and silicon still work. The software is what got worse.</p>

    <h2 id="examples">Enshittification examples: hardware, 2024 to 2026</h2>
    <p>Recent cases where a device people paid for got worse or stopped working because of a software or service decision, and the owner-built fix for each. Newest first. For shutdowns that are announced but haven't happened yet, see the <a href="/death-watch/">Death Watch</a>.</p>
    <ol class="timeline">
      {examples.map((e) => (
        <li>
          <span class="when">{e.when}</span>
          <div>
            <p><b>{e.device}.</b> {e.what}</p>
            <p class="fix"><span class="acc">fix:</span> <a href={`/p/${e.slug}/`}>{e.fix}</a></p>
          </div>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ol>

    <h2>Four ways hardware gets worse after you buy it</h2>

    <h3>1. The cloud goes away</h3>
    <p>Devices that depend on a company server stop working when the server stops. The fix is a local replacement for the missing service.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><a href="/p/trifo-lucy-local-server/">Trifo Lucy local server</a>: a robot vacuum controlled from a home server</li>
      <li><a href="/p/hello-sense-revival/">Hello Sense revival</a>: a sleep tracker rebuilt after its company closed</li>
      <li><a href="/p/smarter-coffee-for-home-assistant/">Smarter Coffee for Home Assistant</a>: a coffee machine whose app went obsolete</li>
      <li><a href="/p/smartthings-washer-driver/">SmartThings washer driver</a>: a Samsung washer cut off from its old app</li>
      <li><a href="/p/sommet/">Sommet</a>: Suunto watches after Movescount shut down</li>
      <li><a href="/p/bose-soundtouch-revival/">Bose SoundTouch revival</a>: presets and internet radio after Bose's cloud shutdown</li>
      <li><a href="/p/roborock-local-server/">Roborock Local Server</a>: Roborock's cloud, run on your own network</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>2. The home screen becomes a shop window</h3>
    <p>Smart TV interfaces fill with promoted content and apps you never asked for. The fix is a different launcher or a careful debloat.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><a href="/p/clean-your-tv/">Clean Your TV</a>: agent-guided debloat for Android TV and Google TV</li>
      <li><a href="/p/arn0/">Arn0</a>: a custom home screen for a Samsung Q70B</li>
      <li><a href="/p/dumbtv/">dumbtv</a>: ads and tracking stripped from Fire TV</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>3. Support ends</h3>
    <p>Drivers stop at an old OS version and operating systems drop older machines. The fix is new software for hardware that still works.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><a href="/p/hp-laser-on-apple-silicon/">HP Laser on Apple Silicon</a>: printers with no current Mac driver</li>
      <li><a href="/p/pixma-rs/">pixma-rs</a>: a Canon scanner macOS doesn't support</li>
      <li><a href="/p/omarchy/">Omarchy</a>: older Intel Macs on Linux</li>
      <li><a href="/p/opticfilm-8200i-linux/">OpticFilm 8200i on Linux</a>: a film scanner with no Linux driver</li>
      <li><a href="/p/ftape-modernized/">ftape, modernised</a>: 1990s tape drives on a current Linux kernel</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>4. One app, one way</h3>
    <p>Some devices only work through the manufacturer's app, on its terms. The fix is another way in.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><a href="/p/respira/">Respira</a>: an embroidery machine driven from a computer</li>
      <li><a href="/p/yotwo/">Yotwo</a>: a Yoto player pointed at your own audiobook library</li>
      <li><a href="/p/cat-printer-no-stock-app/">Cat printer, no stock app</a>: a thermal printer over Bluetooth</li>
      <li><a href="/p/personal-sonos-controller/">Personal Sonos controller</a>: Sonos controls arranged the owner's way</li>
      <li><a href="/p/osmosis-dji-osmo/">Osmosis</a>: DJI cameras without the Mimo app</li>
      <li><a href="/p/opensnek/">OpenSnek</a>: Razer mice configured on a Mac</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Not every case is bad faith. Companies close, priorities change, old products get dropped. The entries on this site describe what broke and what fixed it, and leave motives out.</p>

    <h2>Why this is happening now</h2>
    <p>Undoing it used to take a team with reverse-engineering experience. Coding assistants changed that. Owners now use <a href="/ai/claude-code/">Claude Code</a>, <a href="/ai/chatgpt/">ChatGPT</a> and similar tools to read protocols, decompile apps and write replacement software, on top of years of open-source work by people who did it the hard way.</p>

    <h2>Questions</h2>
    {faq.map(([q, a]) => (<><h3>{q}</h3><p>{a}</p></>))}

    <p><a class="btn" href="/#log">see all projects</a></p>
  </div>
</article>
</Base>
